ns especially exacerbation  of copd and bronchitis  and then a word of caution  and that is the pro-calcitonin in  general  should not be ordered in patients with  renal insufficiency given the fact that  levels tend to be elevated anyway  and it's cleared by the kidney so that  levels may be elevated and it'll be very  difficult to interpret so i just  discourage people from getting it with  renal insufficiency  so i want to end on on this slide  uh and you know the old antigen  antibiotics were miracle drugs you know  when penicillins were first introduced  into clinical practice in the 1940s  this was a huge breakthrough and then  more types of antibiotics became  available and they didn't seem to be  much of a downside risk  however as most of you know we have  fewer new drugs coming on the market we  have increasing antimicrobial systems  which by the way has been accelerated  by covin in a recent cdc report  but the new paradigm is on the right  it's good when they're used well  and better when they're used  thoughtfully i i hate to see what i call  the desert practice of medicine that i  see so frequently in the emergency  department where they give them  vancomycin and piptazo or they give them  vancomycin placefeme  and then they worry about everything  else later now in the severely septic  patients especially septic shock that's  appropriate  but in many of these patients there is  time to get more information before we  decide on prescribing an antibiotic and  that's what i mean by when you use it  thoughtfully take advantage of all the  clinical information is available and if  you have a for instance a procalcitonin  platform in your facility that may be  one bit of additional information you  use  to decide on whether you start  antibiotics or  when you can stop antibiotics so i think  that's important so with that i'm going  to stop now and i hope we have plenty of  time for questions and i hope we  have some questions but i hope i gave  you sort of 